// =============================================================================
// PNG NETWORK TOPOLOGY: PEERED SPOKE (PNG IN THE HUB) VS ISOLATED SPOKE (ITS OWN PNG)
//
// JSONC for you to read; emit PLAIN JSON (strip the // lines) when you write
// architecture.md. Read reference/style-networking.md first.
//
// HOW IT WORKS ON DATABRICKS (use this to adapt the diagram to the user's network):
//   • A Private Network Gateway (PNG) injects a Databricks-managed NIC into a dedicated
//     /28 subnet of ONE customer network. Serverless reaches resources in that network
//     and in networks transitively connected to it, nothing else.
//   • Workspace VNets with VNet injection (public and private subnets for classic
//     compute) are usually spokes. Where the PNG subnet goes depends on whether the spoke
//     is connected to the rest of the network:
//       – PEERED SPOKE: it is peered to the hub, so the PNG subnet sits in the HUB and
//         serverless reaches the hub, its gateway to on-premises, and the peered spokes.
//         The spoke itself needs no PNG subnet.
//       – ISOLATED SPOKE: it is not peered to anything, so a PNG in the hub cannot reach
//         it. It gets its OWN PNG subnet, and serverless then reaches resources inside
//         that VNet only.
//   • Plan the count: 10 NCCs per region per account and 2 PNGs per NCC; the usual
//     split is one NCC per environment and/or business unit.
//
// WHAT IT DEMONSTRATES (layout):
//   • Three boxes in one top-level row: the peered spoke, the hub, the isolated spoke.
//     The only edge is the one peering, so the missing edge to the isolated spoke is the message.
//   • Each PNG subnet carries the same orange border (`style.borderColor`), so the eye
//     finds the two places a PNG can live without a legend.
//   • Subnets are `box`es around one icon; a VNet's subnets sit in one row.
//
// ADAPT IT: name the real VNets; drop the bastion or firewall when the hub has none.
// The hub's connection to on-premises: see png-topology-hub-spoke.jsonc.
// =============================================================================
